BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (  # The Myth of the River and the Rain) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (  In the beginning, the River dwelt in the valley, and the Rain lived in the sky. Each possessed) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (     a precious gift.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (  The Rain held five silver droplets, while the River waited below, empty and yearning. The Rain) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (     faced a choice: hoard the droplets or release them to the River below.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (  When the Rain let the droplets fall, a miracle occurred-each droplet multiplied threefold as i) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (    t descended. The River swelled with fifteen silver drops, suddenly abundant.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (  But the River, too, faced a choice. It could keep all fifteen drops, leaving the Rain depleted) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (     forever. Or it could send some back, returning the gift.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (  The wise River understood: the Rain had made itself vulnerable by giving first. The Rain had c) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (    reated abundance where scarcity lived. So the River sent back seven drops, keeping eight.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (  The Rain now held seven instead of five. The River held eight instead of zero.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (  And so the cycle continued, round after round, each participant sometimes giving, sometimes re) Tj ET
